Okay, let's see. Tom's ship can travel at 10 miles per hour. He sails from 1 PM to 4 PM. So first, I need to figure out how far he went during that time. From 1 to 4 PM is 3 hours, right? So if he's going 10 mph for 3 hours, the distance would be speed multiplied by time. So 10 times 3 is 30 miles. Got that part.

Now, he has to come back, and his speed is 6 mph. The question is, how long does it take him to get back? Well, the distance is the same, 30 miles, but his speed is slower now. So time equals distance divided by speed. So 30 miles divided by 6 mph. Hmm, 30 divided by 6 is 5. So that would be 5 hours. Wait, is there anything else I need to consider? The problem doesn't mention any stops or changes in speed, so I think that's it. So the answer should be 5 hours.
